{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkX2oEsqp27kKMjwi4JL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Jjg9pdSTNfKmDeeKcEZYX7","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with a clef and two flats but no time signature, so it continues 4/4. Bar 1 of the line: [p], a dotted quarter (about Bb4, middle line) under a fermata, then an 8th rest and a half rest. That fills four beats. By position it is bar 135, but the edition has a whole-bar rest there, so the note does not match. Bar 2: a whole rest under a fermata, with \"f\" above. This is bar 136, a rest in the edition. The fermata fits the cue \"Capriccio ad lib. Violino primo\" at the line's end. Then a double bar, 2/4 and \"Come Prima\". Bar 137: 8th rest, [p], then Bb4 C5 C#5 in 8ths. Bar 138: D5 beamed with F#4 G4 A4. The copyist beams the four notes together; the edition has D5 slurred into the bar, then a slur over F#–G–A, with A tied over. Bar 139: tied A4 8th, G4 quarter, F#4 8th. Bar 140: G4 quarter, Bb4 quarter, then the final barline. Bar 141's 16ths are not on this line. So the line runs from bar 135 to bar 140. The only mismatch is bar 135's fermata note.","right":true,"insights":"The 2/4 change and \"Come Prima\" pin bar 137 exactly, so the bars after it are easy to match. Counting back from it gives bars 136 and 135. Bar 135 was hard because the line has a fermata note where the edition has a whole-bar rest. Bar numbers on the page, or an edition that showed the fermatas and the solo cue, would settle the line's first bar."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5246,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5246},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"dia